Cell[TextData[{
 "The entry ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"23", ",", "276"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " indicates that the sum of the first ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["23", TraditionalForm]]],
 " positive integers is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["276", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This still may not be enough to get an idea of what the formula could be, \
in which case a graph of the data can be of use. The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ListPlot",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ListPlot"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function is used to graph functions and data, and was first discussed in \
Section 2.3 of this manual. In this situation, we want to plot the points \
that the previous application of ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Table",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Table"], "FunctionLink"],
 " produced: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"3", ",", "6"}], "}"}], ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 ". To do this, we apply ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ListPlot",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ListPlot"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to the output above. Note that either of the previous two lists can be \
used. Given a list of pairs, ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ListPlot",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ListPlot"], "FunctionLink"],
 " produces the graph interpreting the pairs as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 "-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["y", TraditionalForm]]],
 " coordinate pairs. Given a list of values, the values are interpreted as \
the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["y", TraditionalForm]]],
 " coordinates with the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " coordinate being the position of the value in the list. That is, the first \
element is plotted at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 "-value 1, the second element at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 "-value 2, etc. In this case, the plots are identical, so we will use the \
shorter of the two expressions. Since ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " computes the sums quickly, we'll go out to a maximum of n = 1000. That \
way, we'll be sure to have a good idea of the shape of the graph. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", 
  RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
     RowBox[{"i", ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"i", ",", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"n", ",", "1000"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

