         RowBox[{#2, ",", "After"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]& )]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This gives us a visual comparison of the time complexity of the two \
algorithms. You can see that the second approach significantly outperforms \
the first. It is left to the reader to compare the functions we created to ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[CloseCurlyQuote]s built-in ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["PowerMod",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/PowerMod"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Recursion and Iteration", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section4b"],

Cell["\<\
In this subsection we compare recursive and iterative approaches for \
computing factorial. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Recursive Factorial", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "First we will implement Algorithm 1 from Section 5.4, a recursive algorithm \
for computing ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "!"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". This function accepts a nonnegative integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " as its input. If the input value is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["0", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the function returns ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["1", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Otherwise, it multiplies ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " by the value of the function applied to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"factorialR", "[", "n_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"If", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"n", "\[Equal]", "0"}], ",", "1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"n", "*", 
       RowBox[{"factorialR", "[", 
        RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that, while we could have defined the function\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
action on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "\[NotEqual]", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " without using the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Module",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Module"], "FunctionLink"],
 " structure, a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Module",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Module"], "FunctionLink"],
 " will be needed in the iterative version and in comparing performance of \
algorithms, it is important to keep as much of the implementations the same \
as possible."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We test this function on 10 and verify that it has the same result as ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "'s built-in operator."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"factorialR", "[", "10", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["3628800"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"10", "!"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["3628800"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Iterative Factorial", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We can implement factorial with an iterative algorithm as well. Our \
function will use a variable ",
 StyleBox["f", "Input"],
 ", initialized to 1, to store the value of the factorial. It will compute \
using a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["For",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/For"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop with a loop variable running from 1 to ",
 StyleBox["n", "Input"],
 ". Within the loop, ",
 StyleBox["f", "Input"],
 " will be multiplied by the current value of the loop variable."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"factorialI", "[", "n_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"f", ",", "i"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"f", "=", "1"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"For", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"i", "=", "1"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"i", "++"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"f", "=", 
        RowBox[{"f", "*", "i"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", "f"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",\

 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We again check to make sure the result is correct on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", "10"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"factorialI", "[", "10", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["3628800"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Comparing Recursion and Iteration", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Note that these two algorithms require exactly the same number of \
multiplications. From this point of view, their complexity is the same. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "However, let's look at their performance. We consider values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " from 1 to 1500. We use the same approach as we did for ",
 StyleBox["power1", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["power2", "Input"],
 " above to record and plot the time performance. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"timesR", "=", 
   RowBox[{"Block", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"$RecursionLimit", "=", "Infinity"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"n", ",", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Timing", "[", 
           RowBox[{"factorialR", "[", "n", "]"}], "]"}], "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"n", ",", "1500"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"timesI", "=", 
