    GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {{Center}}, "Rows" -> {{Center}}}, 
    AutoDelete -> False, GridBoxItemSize -> Automatic, 
    BaselinePosition -> {1, 1}]& ),
  Editable->True,
  InterpretationFunction->(RowBox[{"Legended", "[", 
     RowBox[{#, ",", 
       RowBox[{"Placed", "[", 
         RowBox[{#2, ",", "After"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]& )]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "First, you might wonder about the outliers which appear to be values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for which one function or the other performs particularly poorly. These \
are essentially noise resulting from other processes on the computer. They \
are also inconsistent; running the commands again will produce slightly \
different results. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "However, it is clear that, despite the occasional peculiar value, the \
iterative function outperforms the recursive one for large values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". This is in spite of the fact that the two algorithms involve the same \
number of multiplications. Let's consider other sources of potential \
differences in complexity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["For",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/For"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop in the iterative function includes a comparison (",
 StyleBox["i", "Input"],
 " must be tested against ",
 StyleBox["n", "Input"],
 " to determine if the loop continues) and the recursive function makes the \
comparison in the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["If",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/If"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement. So the two functions are effectively equal in terms of the \
number of comparisons used."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The iterative function involves two assignments (the assignment of ",
 StyleBox["f", "Input"],
 " and the implicit assignment of ",
 StyleBox["i", "Input"],
 " when it is incremented) absent from the recursive version. But two \
assignments are much less costly than a function call. Recursive function \
calls require ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to perform several operations in the background, both in order to execute \
the recursive call and to keep track of where ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is in the chain of recursive calls. All of which takes time and memory \
that the iterative approach does not require."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
It is important to keep in mind the cost of recursion. When an iterative \
algorithm is available, it may be more efficient. On the other hand, \
recursive algorithms are often more natural to use and can better reveal the \
mathematical concepts. \
\>", "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Merge Sort", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section4c"],

Cell["We conclude this section by implementing merge sort.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The merge sort algorithm is described in Algorithm 9 of the text. The ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " function will accept as its input a list of integers. Its main function is \
to split the single list it receives into two halves, unless the list \
contains only one element. The output of ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " is the result of applying ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " to both halves of the list and then recombining them with ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The following implements Algorithm 9. Note that the ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " function will be written next. For now, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " simply accepts ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " as a symbol."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", 
   RowBox[{"L", ":", 
    RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"m", ",", "L1", ",", "L2"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"If", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], ">", "1"}], ",", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"m", "=", 
        RowBox[{"Floor", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], "/", "2"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
        RowBox[{"L", "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", 
          RowBox[{";;", "m"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
        RowBox[{"L", "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"m", "+", "1"}], ";;"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"merge", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", "L1", "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", "L2", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", "else", "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "L"}], 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",\

 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note the use of the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Span",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Span"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox[";;", "Input"],
 ") operator to determine the two sublists. The syntax ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["a", "CodeVar"], ";;", 
   StyleBox["b", "CodeVar"]}]], "myCode"],
 ", within the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Part",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Part"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["[[\[Ellipsis]]]", "Input"],
 ") operator, is used to obtain the sublist of elements from position ",
 StyleBox["a", "CodeVar"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["b", "CodeVar"],
 ". Omitting ",
 StyleBox["a", "CodeVar"],
 ", as in ",
 StyleBox[";;5", "Input"],
 ", obtains the elements from the beginning to position 5. And omitting ",
 StyleBox["b", "CodeVar"],
 ", as in ",
 StyleBox["3;;", "Input"],
 ", obtains the elements from position 3 to the end of the list."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Implementing Merge", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To complete the function, we need to define ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 ". The ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " function accepts two lists, which it assumes are sorted, and returns a \
single list that contains all the elements of both of the inputs and is \
sorted. The procedure is described in Algorithm 10 of the text."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In implementing ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 ", the first step is to duplicate the input lists. This is because the lists \
are emptied of their elements as the ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " proceeds, and arguments to a function cannot be modified. We also \
initialize the list that will be the result to the empty list."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The main work of ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " is contained in a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["While",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/While"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop conditioned on both lists being nonempty. Within this ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["While",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/While"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop there are an ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["If",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/If"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement and a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Which",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Which"], "FunctionLink"],
 ". The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["If",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/If"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement will implement the instruction to \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]remove \
smaller of first elements of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " from its list; put it at the right end of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 "\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] from Algorithm 10. This ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["If",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/If"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement will test whether the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is smaller than the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". If so, then the first element is removed from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and added to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". If not, in the else clause, the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is moved to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["AppendTo",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/AppendTo"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to add elements to the end of the list ",
 StyleBox["L", "Input"],
 ". Note that ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["AppendTo",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/AppendTo"], "FunctionLink"],
 ", when applied to the name of a list and an element, automatically replaces \
the list stored in the symbol. We use ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Delete",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Delete"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to remove the first element from the list. ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Delete",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Delete"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is applied to a list and a position and returns the list with the element \
in that position removed. Unlike ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["AppendTo",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/AppendTo"], "FunctionLink"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Delete",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Delete"], "FunctionLink"],
 " does not automatically update a symbol representing a list, so we must \
reassign the name of the list to the result of the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Delete",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Delete"], "FunctionLink"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Which",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Which"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement will implement the if statement found in Algorithm 10. The first \
condition will be that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty, and if so the remainder of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " will be added to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " will be emptied. In a second condition, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " will be tested. Note that the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Which",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Which"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[CloseCurlyQuote]s answer to the else-if construction. ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Join",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Join"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is used to combine two lists."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is the implementation of ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"merge", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"l1", ":", 
     RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"l2", ":", 
     RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"L", "=", 
       RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"L1", "=", "l1"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"L2", "=", "l2"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"While", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"L1", "=!=", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ")"}], "&&", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"L2", "=!=", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ")"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"If", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L1", "[", 
            RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}], "<", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "[", 
            RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"AppendTo", "[", 
            RowBox[{"L", ",", 
             RowBox[{"L1", "[", 
              RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
           "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
           RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Delete", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L1", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          
          RowBox[{"(*", "else", "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"AppendTo", "[", 
            RowBox[{"L", ",", 
             RowBox[{"L2", "[", 
              RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
           "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Delete", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L2", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"Which", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"L1", "===", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L", ",", "L2"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
            RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{"L2", "===", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L", ",", "L1"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
           RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
            RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]}], 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "L"}]}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We apply ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " to a list as follows."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"7", ",", "4", ",", "1", ",", "5", ",", "2", ",", "3", ",", "6"}], 
   "}"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "3", ",", "4", ",", "5", ",", "6", ",", "7"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Tracing Merge Sort", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To better understand how functions work, it is sometimes useful to create \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]verbose\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] versions of them. That \
is, versions of the functions that use ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Print",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Print"], "FunctionLink"],
 " or other means to report on what it is that they are doing. We provide a \
verbose version of ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " and apply it to the list ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"3", ",", "1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"mergeSortVerbose", "[", 
   RowBox[{"L", ":", 
    RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"m", ",", "L1", ",", "L2"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
      RowBox[{"\"\<mergeSort called on \>\"", ",", "L"}], "]"}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"If", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], ">", "1"}], ",", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"m", "=", 
         RowBox[{"Floor", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], "/", "2"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
         RowBox[{"\"\<m=\>\"", ",", "m"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        
        RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
         RowBox[{"L", "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", 
           RowBox[{";;", "m"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
         RowBox[{"\"\<L1=\>\"", ",", "L1"}], "]"}], ";", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
         RowBox[{"L", "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"m", "+", "1"}], ";;"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
         RowBox[{"\"\<L2=\>\"", ",", "L2"}], "]"}], ";", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"merge", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"mergeSortVerbose", "[", "L1", "]"}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"mergeSortVerbose", "[", "L2", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"(*", "else", "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Print", "[", "\"\<length=1\>\"", "]"}], ";", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", "L"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"mergeSortVerbose", "[", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"3", ",", "1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"mergeSort called on \"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"3", ",", "1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["mergeSort called on ", {3, 1, 2}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"m=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", "1"}],
  SequenceForm["m=", 1],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"L1=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["L1=", {3}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"L2=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["L2=", {1, 2}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"mergeSort called on \"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["mergeSort called on ", {3}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"length=1\"\>"], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"mergeSort called on \"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["mergeSort called on ", {1, 2}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"m=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", "1"}],
  SequenceForm["m=", 1],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"L1=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["L1=", {1}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"L2=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["L2=", {2}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"mergeSort called on \"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["mergeSort called on ", {1}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"length=1\"\>"], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"mergeSort called on \"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}],
  SequenceForm["mergeSort called on ", {2}],
  Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"length=1\"\>"], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "3"}], "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We recommend reading through the output. Then try it with a larger example, \
say with 7 elements, to make sure that you understand how merge sort works. \
You can also create a verbose version of ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " to see the details of how that function works."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["5.5 Program Correctness", "Section",
 CellTags->"Section5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this section we will prove the correctness of the merge sort program \
that we implemented in the last section. This will require that we prove the \
correctness of ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " as well as ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 ". We begin with ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["merge", "Input"]], "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section5a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For convenience, we will repeat the definition of ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 ". Also, we've added comments to indicate where we've broken the procedure \
into three segments: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"merge", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"l1", ":", 
     RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"l2", ":", 
     RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"L", ",", "L1", ",", "L2"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"(*", 
     RowBox[{"begin", " ", "S1"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"L", "=", 
      RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"L1", "=", "l1"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"L2", "=", "l2"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"(*", 
      RowBox[{"end", " ", "S1"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"While", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"L1", "=!=", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ")"}], "&&", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"L2", "=!=", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ")"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"(*", 
        RowBox[{"begin", " ", "S2"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"If", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L1", "[", 
            RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}], "<", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "[", 
            RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"AppendTo", "[", 
            RowBox[{"L", ",", 
             RowBox[{"L1", "[", 
              RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
           "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
           RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Delete", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L1", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          
          RowBox[{"(*", "else", "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"AppendTo", "[", 
            RowBox[{"L", ",", 
             RowBox[{"L2", "[", 
              RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
           "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Delete", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L2", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"(*", 
         RowBox[{"end", " ", "S2"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"(*", 
         RowBox[{"begin", " ", "S3"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"Which", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"L1", "===", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L", ",", "L2"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
           RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
            RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{"L2", "===", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"L", "=", 
            RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
             RowBox[{"L", ",", "L1"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
           RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
            RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]}], 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{"end", " ", "S3"}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", "L"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " (the inputs to the function) are ordered, nonempty, and disjoint lists of \
integers. Let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the proposition that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " (the output) is an ordered list and that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"L", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as sets (that is, the set of integers appearing in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is equal to the union of the set of integers appearing in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the set of integers in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "). We claim that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["merge", "Input"], "}"}], "q"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", i.e., that ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " is partially correct with respect to the initial condition ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the final assertion ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["q", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the proposition that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["l", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", "2"], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the empty list. It is clear that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"p", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "1"], "}"}], "p"}], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["q", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Define the following propositional variables:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the proposition that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "; that is, the set of elements appearing in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a subset of the set of elements appearing in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and the order of the elements in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the same as their order in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Note that it immediately follows from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the proposition that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " (and thus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered)."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"L", "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as sets."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the proposition that all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " are smaller than all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". That is, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"\[ForAll]", 
      RowBox[{"x", "\[Element]", "L"}]}], ")"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"\[ForAll]", 
      RowBox[{"y", "\[Element]", 
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[Union]", 
        SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}]}]}], ")"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"x", "<", "y"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the proposition ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["r", "1"], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["r", "2"], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["r", "3"], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["r", "4"], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["r", "5"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We claim that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"p", "\[And]", 
     SubscriptBox["q", "1"]}], "\[Rule]", "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["q", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". That is, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are ordered, nonempty, and disjoint lists of integers. Also, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["l", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", "2"], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty. Then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " hold since a list is a sublist of itself. Proposition ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty and thus is ordered vacuously. That ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is true follows from substituting ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[EmptySet]", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". And ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is vacuous since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty. From ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"p", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "1"], "}"}], "p"}], "\[And]", 
    SubscriptBox["q", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"p", "\[And]", 
      SubscriptBox["q", "1"]}], ")"}], "\[Rule]", "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", we have ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "1"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Next, we will show that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a loop invariant for the loop ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"while", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[NotEqual]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", "}"}], " ", "and", " ", 
        SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}], "\[NotEqual]", 
       RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ")"}], 
     SubscriptBox["S", "2"]}], ";", 
    SubscriptBox["S", "3"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Denote by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["c", TraditionalForm]]],
 " the condition ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[NotEqual]", 
     RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "2"], "\[NotEqual]", 
     RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". We must show that if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["c", TraditionalForm]]],
 " hold, then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is true after ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["S", "2"], ";", 
    SubscriptBox["S", "3"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is executed. First we will show ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{"c", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "2"], "}"}], "r"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and then that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "3"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To show that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{"c", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "2"], "}"}], "r"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", "c"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". That is, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"L", "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " are smaller than every member of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Also, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are nonempty. Then both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " have first elements. Assume that the if condition of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds. That is, the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is smaller than the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Then the two commands in the then clause of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are executed: the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is added to the end of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " has its first element removed."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We need to show that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds following the execution of the then clause of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": the new ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of the old ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " since an element was removed meaning that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], "\[Subset]", 
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"1", "old"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as sets and the order of the remaining elements was not modified. Since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " which was a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the new ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": the new ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is identical is the old ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and thus remains a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": the old ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was ordered, and, since we assume that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " held before execution of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", every element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " was smaller than every element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], "\[Union]", 
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"2", "old"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". In particular, the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " was larger than all elements of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". And so ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "new"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": The smallest element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " was removed from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and added to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Thus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "new"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"1", "new"}]]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "old"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"1", "old"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and hence ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"L", "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": We must show that all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "new"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are smaller than all members of both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"2", "new"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be an arbitrary element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "new"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Either ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was a member of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". If ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was a member of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " then the assumption that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " held before execution of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " guarantees that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is smaller than all elements of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], "\[Union]", 
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"2", "new"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". On the other hand, assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was the first element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", it is ordered and thus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was also the smallest element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and hence is less than all elements of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Also, by the assumption that the if condition of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " evaluated true, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is smaller than the first (and smallest) element of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"2", "old"}]], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"2", "new"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as well. So ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is smaller than all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], "\[Union]", 
    SubscriptBox["L", 
     RowBox[{"2", "new"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The above shows that if the if condition of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds, then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds after executing the then clause. In case the condition fails and the \
else clause executes, the proof is similar. We conclude that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{"c", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "2"], "}"}], "r"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Next we will show that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "3"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds. Consider the case that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty. Then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is appended onto the end of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is set to the empty list."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is, since we assume the if condition, empty and thus a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": the second statement in the then clause sets ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " equal to the empty list, which is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": we assume that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered, that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"2", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a sublist of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["l", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and thus is ordered, and that all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are smaller than all members of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"2", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Thus, adding ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", 
    RowBox[{"2", "old"}]], TraditionalForm]]],
 " to the end of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "old"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " to produce ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "new"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " results in an ordered list."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": as sets, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["L", "new"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "old"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"2", "old"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", so ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["L", "new"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"1", "new"}]], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"2", "new"}]]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["L", "old"], "\[Union]", 
       SubscriptBox["L", 
        RowBox[{"2", "old"}]]}], ")"}], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", 
      RowBox[{"1", "old"}]], "\[Union]", "\[EmptySet]"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". This is equal to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
    SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " by the assumption that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " held before execution."
}], "Item1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": after execution of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["S", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are empty and assertion ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "5"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is true vacuously."
}], "Item1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The case that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is empty is similar. Thus, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "3"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We have shown ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{"c", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "2"], "}"}], "r"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "3"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and thus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "\[And]", 
    RowBox[{"c", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["S", "2"], ";", 
       SubscriptBox["S", "3"]}], "}"}], "r"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Hence ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a loop invariant for the while loop. By the inference rule for while \
loops, we have that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"while", " ", "c", " ", 
       SubscriptBox["S", "2"]}], ";", 
      SubscriptBox["S", "3"]}], "}"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"\[Not]", "c"}], "\[And]", "r"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Combining this result with the conclusion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     SubscriptBox["S", "1"], "}"}], "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " from the paragraph immediately following the definition of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", we have that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["merge", "Input"], "}"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"\[Not]", "c"}], "\[And]", "r"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We conclude by claiming ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\[Not]", "c"}], "\[And]", "r"}], "\[Rule]", "q"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Recall that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 " was the final assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"L", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as sets. Assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[Not]", "c"}], "\[And]", "r"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". That ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ordered is the claim of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "3"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". By ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["r", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", we have that, as sets, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"L", "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["L", "2"]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". But ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Not]", "c"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " implies that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are empty. Hence, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"L", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["l", "1"], "\[Union]", 
     SubscriptBox["l", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Thus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds and we have completed the proof that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["merge", "Input"], "}"}], "q"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and hence ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " is partially correct."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"]], "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section5b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now we turn to the ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " function. We repeat its definition below."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", 
   RowBox[{"L", ":", 
    RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"m", ",", "L1", ",", "L2"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"If", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], ">", "1"}], ",", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"m", "=", 
        RowBox[{"Floor", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Length", "[", "L", "]"}], "/", "2"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"L1", "=", 
        RowBox[{"L", "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", 
          RowBox[{";;", "m"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"L2", "=", 
        RowBox[{"L", "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"m", "+", "1"}], ";;"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"merge", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", "L1", "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"mergeSort", "[", "L2", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", "else", "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "L"}], 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a nonempty list of distinct integers, and let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the assertion that the function returns a list which has the same \
elements as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and is ordered. Our claim is that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"], "}"}], "q"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Since ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " is recursive, our proof will be by strong induction on the length of the \
list ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For the basis case, assume that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " has only one element. Also assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Then the if condition of ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " fails and the program terminates by returning ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " unmodified. But since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " has only one element, it is trivially ordered. Thus, under the basis \
assumption that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " has only one element, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"], "}"}], "q"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For the inductive case, we make the inductive assumption that for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"k", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", if a list has length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]]],
 " then ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " returns the list sorted. Assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " has ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " elements. Also assume ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Under these assumptions, the if condition is satisfied."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first command in the then clause assigns ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"m", "=", 
    RowBox[{"\[LeftFloor]", 
     FractionBox[
      RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}], "2"], "\[RightFloor]"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Note that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"m", "<", 
    RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and, since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", ">", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"m", ">", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". All of the inequalities are strict."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next two commands assign ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " to the list consisting of the first ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " elements in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " to the remainder. Note that since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "<", "m", "<", 
    RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", both of these lists are nonempty with at most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " elements."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the final statement of the if clause, ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " is applied to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Since these two lists both have length at most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the inductive assumption implies that the results of ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are lists with the same elements and ordered. Since ",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " is partially correct, as shown in the previous subsection, the result of \
",
 StyleBox["merge", "Input"],
 " is an ordered list consisting of the elements of its input lists. Hence, \
the result of ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " is an ordered list consisting of the same elements as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". That is, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 " holds."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This concludes the inductive step and we conclude ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"], "}"}], "q"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for all lengths of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Hence, ",
 StyleBox["mergeSort", "Input"],
 " is partially correct."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Solutions to Computer Projects and Computations and Explorations\
\>", "Section",
 CellTags->"Solutions"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computer Projects 2", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"SolutionsCP2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Generate all well-formed formulae for expressions involving the variables \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["y", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["z", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the operators \:fffc ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"+", 
     RowBox[{",", 
      RowBox[{"\[CenterDot]", 
       RowBox[{",", 
        RowBox[{"/", 
         RowBox[{",", "-"}]}]}]}]}]}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " or fewer symbols."
}], "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " This problem asks us to not only generate well-formed formulae, but to \
generate ",
 StyleBox["all",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " such formulae subject to a limitation on the number of symbols."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
To begin, we present a recursive definition of the set of well-formed \
formulae on the symbols.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Basis step: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["y", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["z", TraditionalForm]]],
 " are well-formed formulae."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{24, 10}, {3, 0}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Recursive step: If ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["F", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["G", TraditionalForm]]],
 " are well-formed formulae, then so are: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"-", "F"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"F", "+", "G"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"F", "-", "G"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"F", "\[CenterDot]", "G"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"F", "/", "G"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{24, 10}, {3, 0}}],

Cell["\<\
Note that we will fully parenthesize the well-formed formulae so as to avoid \
ambiguity, but parentheses will not be considered symbols for the purpose of \
counting symbols in the formula.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Also note that we will implement the well-formed formulae as strings, not \
as algebraic expressions. The reason for this is that if we build algebraic \
expressions, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " will perform unwanted simplification. For example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"-", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"-", "x"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a well-formed formula distinct from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", but if we enter ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"-", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"-", "x"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as a ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " expression, it will be simplified to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We will approach this problem in two steps. First, we generate well-formed \
formulae using a function with sufficiently many applications of the \
recursive step to guarantee that every well-formed formula of length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " or less is produced. Second, we will prune the well-formed formulae with \
greater than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols. This will leave us with all well-formed formulae involving at \
most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Generating Formulae", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first step is to generate well-formed formulae. For this, we will \
create a pair of functions, similar to ",
 StyleBox["allStrings", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["buildStrings", "Input"],
 " from Section 5.3 of this manual."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The function ",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 " will accept a single argument, a list of well-formed formulae. It will \
apply the recursive step to the existing set. The function first makes a copy \
of the input set, since arguments cannot be modified. Second, using a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Do",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Do"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop over the input set, it applies unary negation. Then, with a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Do",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Do"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop over the input set and over the binary operations, the function adds \
the rest of the well-formed formulae."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"buildWFFs", "[", "S_List", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"T", "=", "S"}], ",", "f", ",", "g", ",", "o"}], "}"}], ",", 
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Do", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"T", "=", 
        RowBox[{"Union", "[", 
         RowBox[{"T", ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"\"\<(-\>\"", "<>", "f", "<>", "\"\<)\>\""}], "}"}]}], 
         "]"}]}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"f", ",", "S"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"Do", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"T", "=", 
        RowBox[{"Union", "[", 
         RowBox[{"T", ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{
           "\"\<(\>\"", "<>", "f", "<>", "o", "<>", "g", "<>", "\"\<)\>\""}], 
           "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"f", ",", "S"}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"g", ",", "S"}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"o", ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
          "\"\<+\>\"", ",", "\"\<-\>\"", ",", "\"\<*\>\"", ",", "\"\</\>\""}],
           "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "T"}]}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let's confirm that this works as expected by applying it to the basis set \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\""}], "}"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"buildWFFs", "[", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\""}], "}"}], 
  "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"(-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"x\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x*x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x+x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x/x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x*y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x-y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x+y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x/y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x*z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x-z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x+z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x/z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(-y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"y\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y*x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y-x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y+x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y*y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y-y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y+y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y/y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y*z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y+z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y/z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"z\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z*x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z+x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z*y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z-y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z+y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z/y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z*z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z+z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z/z)\"\>"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that the order is not the order in which the well-formed formulae are \
added, it is the order that ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Union",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Union"], "FunctionLink"],
 " imposes on the set."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The other component is the function that calls ",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 ". This is nearly identical to ",
 StyleBox["allStrings", "Input"],
 ". ",
 StyleBox["allWFFs", "Input"],
 " accepts a positive integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " representing the number of applications of the recursive step that are to \
be performed. It initializes the set of formulae to the basis set and applies \
",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 " as many times as is called for."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"allWFFs", "[", "m_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"S", ",", "i"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"S", "=", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\""}], "}"}]}], 
     ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"For", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"i", "=", "1"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[LessEqual]", "m"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"i", "++"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"S", "=", 
        RowBox[{"buildWFFs", "[", "S", "]"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "S"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now the question is: how many applications of the recursive step are needed \
to be sure that the result contains all well-formed formulae of length at \
most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "? Clearly, 0 applications of ",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 " are needed to obtain all formulae consisting of 1 symbol, as this is the \
basis step. Also, the formulae produced by an application of ",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 " contain at least one symbol more than was present in the previous step \
(from the unary negation). So after ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " applications of ",
 StyleBox["buildWFFs", "Input"],
 ", we are guaranteed to have all well-formed formulae with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols or fewer."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To illustrate, we will find all well-formed formulae of length at most 3. \
Apply ",
 StyleBox["AllWFFs", "Input"],
 " to 2."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"allWFF3", "=", 
   RowBox[{"allWFFs", "[", "2", "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"],

Cell["We suppressed the output since the output would be lengthy.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Length", "[", "allWFF3", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["7101"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Here is the list of every 300th formula. ", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"allWFF3", "[", 
    RowBox[{"[", "i", "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"i", ",", "1", ",", "7101", ",", "300"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"(-(-x))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((x-x)-(-y))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((x*x)*(z-x))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((x-y)+(x-x))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((x*y)-(y-y))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((x/y)*(z-y))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((x*z)+(x-z))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((x/z)-(y-z))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((-y)+(x+x))\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y-(x+y))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((y+x)*(z+x))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((y/y)+(x+x))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((y+y)-(y+y))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((y-y)*(z+z))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((y+z)+(x+z))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((y-z)+(-z))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((z+x)/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"((z-x)-(y/x))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((z*x)*(z/y))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((z-y)+(x/y))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((z*y)-(y/z))\"\>", ",", "\<\"((z/y)*(z/z))\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"((-z)+(z/y))\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z-(z/z))\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Note that these involve up to seven symbols. Since we want the formulae with \
at most three symbols, we must remove from this set all the formulae with \
more than three. For this, we will need a function that calculates the number \
of symbols in a formula. \
\>", "Text"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Pruning the Set", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To count the number of characters in a string, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " provides the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringLength",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringLength"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. If you apply ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringLength",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringLength"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to a string, the command returns the number of characters in the string."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringLength", "[", "\"\<abcde\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["5"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "However, the number of symbols in a well-formed formula is not equal to its \
length, since parentheses are not considered symbols. Fortunately, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " also provides the function ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringCount",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringCount"], "FunctionLink"],
 ". This function takes two arguments. The first is the string. The second is \
a substring or a list of substrings that you wish to count within the first. \
For example, to determine the number of a\[CloseCurlyQuote]s and b\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s within the string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]abcccbabbcab\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], you enter the following."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringCount", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<abcccbabbcab\>\"", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<a\>\"", ",", "\"\<b\>\""}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["8"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To count the number of symbols in a well-formed formula, we apply ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringCount",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringCount"], "FunctionLink"],
 " with second argument the list of all symbols: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
    "\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\"", ",", "\"\<+\>\"", ",", 
     "\"\<-\>\"", ",", "\"\<*\>\"", ",", "\"\</\>\""}], "}"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 ". For example, the number of symbols in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\"\<((z-x)-(z*x))\>\"", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringCount", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<((z-x)-(z*x))\>\"", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
    "\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\"", ",", "\"\<+\>\"", ",", 
     "\"\<-\>\"", ",", "\"\<*\>\"", ",", "\"\</\>\""}], "}"}]}], 
  "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["7"], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In order to prune the set ",
 StyleBox["allWFF3", "Input"],
 " so that it contains only the formulae with 3 or fewer symbols, we\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Select",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Select"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Select",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Select"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function can be used to find the subset of a given set consisting of those \
elements satisfying a given condition. ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Select",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Select"], "FunctionLink"],
 " requires two arguments. The first is the original list. The second is a \
boolean-valued function of one argument that can be applied to the elements \
of the list and returns ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["True",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/True"], "FunctionLink"],
 " for those elements that should be selected. The result is the list of \
elements of the original list for which the function returned true. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In our case, the Boolean-valued function should return true if the \
well-formed formula has three or fewer symbols. We will create a function to \
test the result of ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringCount",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringCount"], "FunctionLink"],
 " against 3."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"testWFF3", "[", "s_String", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"StringCount", "[", 
    RowBox[{"s", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "\"\<x\>\"", ",", "\"\<y\>\"", ",", "\"\<z\>\"", ",", "\"\<+\>\"", ",", 
       "\"\<-\>\"", ",", "\"\<*\>\"", ",", "\"\</\>\""}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "<=",
    "3"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We can now use the name of this function as the second argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Select",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Select"], "FunctionLink"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Select", "[", 
  RowBox[{"allWFF3", ",", "testWFF3"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"(-(-x))\"\>", ",", "\<\"(-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"x\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x*x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x+x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x*y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x-y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x+y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x/y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x*z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(x-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x+z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(x/z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(-(-y))\"\>", ",", "\<\"(-y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"y\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y*x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y+x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y*y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y-y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y+y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y/y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y*z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(y-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y+z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(y/z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(-(-z))\"\>", ",", "\<\"(-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"z\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z*x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z-x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z+x)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z/x)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z*y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z-y)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z+y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z/y)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z*z)\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"(z-z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z+z)\"\>", ",", "\<\"(z/z)\"\>"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It is left to the reader to generalize and combine these functions into a \
single function that accepts ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " as an argument and returns the list of all well-formed formulae with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " or fewer symbols."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computations and Explorations 2", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"SolutionsCE2"],

Cell["\<\
Determine which Fibonacci numbers are divisible by 5, which are divisible by \
7, and which are divisible by 11. Prove that your conjectures are correct. \
\>", "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " We use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Fibonacci",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Fibonacci"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function to determine the Fibonacci numbers. This function applied to an \
integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " returns the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "th Fibonacci number."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To answer the first part of the question, we want to know which Fibonacci \
numbers are divisible by 5. That is, we want to determine for which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "th Fibonacci divisible by 5. We will construct a list consisting of those \
indices between 1 and 50 for which the corresponding Fibonacci number is \
divisible by 5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Reap", "[", 
  RowBox[{"For", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"n", "=", "1"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"n", "\[LessEqual]", "50"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"n", "++"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"If", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Divisible", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Fibonacci", "[", "n", "]"}], ",", "5"}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"Sow", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}],
   "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"Null", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
     "5", ",", "10", ",", "15", ",", "20", ",", "25", ",", "30", ",", "35", 
      ",", "40", ",", "45", ",", "50"}], "}"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This list suggests that the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "th Fibonacci number is divisible by 5 when ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is. To obtain more evidence, we'll design a function to look for \
counterexamples to the assertion: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 5 if and only if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We first create a function that accepts a positive integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and checks whether or not ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a counterexample of the assertion that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 5 if and only if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 5. If either ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is not divisible by 5 when ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is, or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 5 when ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is not, then this function will print a message to that effect. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"checkFib5", "[", "n_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "F", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"F", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Fibonacci", "[", "n", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"If", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Divisible", "[", 
         RowBox[{"F", ",", "5"}], "]"}], "&&", 
        RowBox[{"Not", "[", 
         RowBox[{"Divisible", "[", 
          RowBox[{"n", ",", "5"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"\"\<Fn=\>\"", "F"}], ",", 
         "\"\< is not divisible by 5, but n=\>\"", ",", "n", ",", 
         "\"\< is\>\""}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"If", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Divisible", "[", 
         RowBox[{"n", ",", "5"}], "]"}], "&&", 
        RowBox[{"Not", "[", 
         RowBox[{"Divisible", "[", 
          RowBox[{"F", ",", "5"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"\"\<Fn=\>\"", "F"}], ",", 
         "\"\< is divisible by 5, but n=\>\"", ",", "n", ",", 
         "\"\< is not\>\""}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], ";"}]}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In order to check as many Fibonacci numbers as possible, while not spending \
too much time on the process, we will apply the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["TimeConstrained",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/TimeConstrained"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. The first argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["TimeConstrained",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/TimeConstrained"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is an expression to be evaluated. In this case, the expression will be an \
infinite ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["For",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/For"], "FunctionLink"],
 " loop that applies ",
 StyleBox["checkFib5", "Input"],
 ". We make the loop infinite by entering ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["True",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/True"], "FunctionLink"],
 " for the test. The second argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["TimeConstrained",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/TimeConstrained"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is the time, in seconds, that the expression should be allowed to run. ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["TimeConstrained",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/TimeConstrained"], "FunctionLink"],
 " also accepts a third, optional, argument, that is evaluated if the first \
expression fails to finish. If the third argument is not given, the output in \
case the time runs out will be ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["$Aborted",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/$Aborted"], "FunctionLink"],
 ". We will use a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Print",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Print"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement as the third argument to report the largest value of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "n"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " was checked. Note that we subtract one from the value of the loop variable \
since we cannot be certain whether the current value was in fact checked or \
if it was aborted."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Below, we allow the test to run for 3 seconds.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"TimeConstrained", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"For", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"n", "=", "1"}], ",", "True", ",", 
     RowBox[{"n", "++"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"checkFib5", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", "3", ",", 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{"Print", "[", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<Checked through n=\>\"", ",", 
     RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{"\<\"Checked through n=\"\>", "\[InvisibleSpace]", "58275"}],
  SequenceForm["Checked through n=", 58275],
  Editable->False]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
How many Fibonacci numbers can be checked will depend on your computer. \
Proving the conjecture, as well as forming and proving conjectures for 7 and \
11, is left to the reader.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exercises", "Section",
 CellTags->"Exercises"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to find and prove formulas for the sum of the first ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]]],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "th powers of positive integers for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"n", "=", "4"}], ",", "5", ",", "6", ",", "7", ",", "8", ",", "9",
     ",", "10"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For what positive integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["n", "k"], "-", "n"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " divisible by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for all positive integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "?"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to help you find and prove the formulas sought in Exercises 9, 10, and 11 \
of Section 5.1 of the text. Do not use the symbolic capabilities of the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Sum",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Sum"], "FunctionLink"],
 " command to form your conjectures."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Find integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["d", TraditionalForm]]],
 " such that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["d", TraditionalForm]]],
 " divides ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["a", 
     RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}]], "+", 
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"a", "+", "1"}], ")"}], 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"2", "n"}], "-", "1"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for all positive integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". (Exercises 36 and 37 in Section 5.1 indicate that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"a", "=", "4"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"d", "=", "21"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"a", "=", "11"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"d", "=", "133"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " are two such pairs.)"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Supplementary Exercises 4 and 5 suggest a more general conjecture. Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "'s ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Sum",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Sum"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function to produce evidence for this conjecture."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use the ",
 StyleBox["postageBasis", "Input"],
 " algorithm from Section 5.2 of this manual to find the smallest ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " such that every amount of postage of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " cents or more can be made from stamps worth 78 cents and $5.95."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Write a function that accepts two stamp denominations as input and returns \
the smallest ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " such that every amount of postage of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " cents or more can be made from the given denominations, or returns ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["$Failed",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/$Failed"], "FunctionLink"],
 " if it cannot find such an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Use your function to make a conjecture that describes for which pairs of \
denominations such an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " exists and for which there is no such ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Write a function to recursively build a set from the following definition: \
basis step: 2 and 3 belong to the set; recursive step: if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["y", TraditionalForm]]],
 " are members, then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"x", "\[CenterDot]", "y"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a member."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Timing",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Timing"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function to compare the performance of ",
 StyleBox["gR", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["gF", "Input"],
 " from Section 5.3. Graph the time performance for the two functions. (Be \
sure to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Clear",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Clear"], "FunctionLink"],
 " ",
 StyleBox["gR", "Input"],
 " prior to every execution so that the comparison is fair.)"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Write a function that accepts two stamp denominations and returns all \
amounts of postage that can be paid with up to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " stamps."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The solution provided for Computer Projects 2 is inefficient as a means of \
finding all well-formed formulae involving at most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols. This is because formulae which already have more symbols than the \
maximum are retained and used to produce even longer formulae. In particular, \
after ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " iterations, the resulting set includes formulae of up to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", 
     RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}]], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols (but not all such formulae). Modify the approach taken in the \
solution to Computer Projects 2 so that strings that include more than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " symbols are pruned at each step of the recursion."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Write a function to compute the number of partitions of a positive integer \
(see Exercise 47 in Section 5.3 of the text).\
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Write a function to compute Ackermann's function (see the prelude to Exercise \
48 in Section 5.3).\
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Implement Algorithm 3 for computing ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"gcd", "(", 
    RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " from Section 5.4 of the text."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Implement Algorithm 5, the recursive linear search algorithm, from Section \
5.4 of the text. \
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Implement Algorithm 6, the recursive binary search algorithm, from Section \
5.4 of the text. \
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Compare the performance of your implementations of Algorithm 5 and \
Algorithm 6 as follows: for a variety of values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["L", TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the list of integers from 1 to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Randomly choose one hundred integers between 1 and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and measure the average of the times taken for each algorithm to find the \
randomly chosen integers. Graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " versus the average times. (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"RandomInteger", "[", "{"}], 
   StyleBox["min", "CodeVar"], ",", 
   StyleBox["max", "CodeVar"], "}", ",", 
   StyleBox["count", "CodeVar"], "]"}]], "myCode"],
 " will output a list of ",
 StyleBox["count", "CodeVar"],
 " randomly chosen integers between ",
 StyleBox["min", "CodeVar"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["max", "CodeVar"],
 ".)"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Create three functions to compute Fibonacci numbers: an iterative function, a \
recursive function that stores values, and a recursive function that does not \
store values. Base your functions on Algorithms 7 and 8 in Section 5.4 of the \
text. Create a graph illustrating the time performance of the three functions.\
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Implement quick sort, described in the prelude to Exercise 50 in Section 5.4 \
of the text. Compare the performance with the merge sort implemented in this \
manual. \
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell["\<\
Implement the algorithm described in Supplementary Exercise 44 for expressing \
a rational number as a sum of Egyptian fractions. \
\>", "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to study the McCarthy 91 function. (See the prelude to problem 45 in the \
Supplementary Exercises of Chapter 5.)"
}], "Item1Exercise"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]]
},
